      "id": 1,
      "text": "Jamestown was founded in 1826 as the seat of the newly formed Russell County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jamestown%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was briefly named Jacksonville in honor of President Andrew Jackson before the anti‑Jacksonian party renamed it Jamestown the same year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jamestown%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Greasy Creek’s 150‑foot drop powered early mills, including the Old Farmer’s Woolen Mill founded before 1824 by English textile immigrants.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Jamestown_Kentucky",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Jamestown’s library is a branch of the Russell County Public Library, serving the town’s residents and visitors alike.",
